09:51:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: They are almost certainly using “π is infinite” as shorthand for: > “π has infinitely many digits after the decimal point.” That is common classroom phrasing, but mathematically it is imprecise. A more accurate statement is: > π is a **finite irrational number** with an **infinite, non-repeating decimal expansion**. Teachers may say “pi goes on forever” because its decimal digits do: \[ \pi = 3.141592653589793\ldots \] But the value itself stays between 3 and 4 forever. Adding more digits does not make it grow: - \(3.1\) - \(3.14\) - \(3.141\) - \(3.1415\) These are increasingly accurate approximations approaching one fixed finite value, π. Compare: \[ \frac{1}{3}=0.3333\ldots \] No one would say one-third is an infinite *length* or quantity—it is less than 1. Yet its decimal representation also never ends. If π were actually infinite, then a circle with a finite diameter would have an infinite circumference: \[ C = \pi d. \] A 1-inch diameter circle would have an infinitely long edge, which clearly is not what geometry means. Its circumference is about 3.14159 inches: finite, just not expressible exactly with a terminating or repeating decimal.
