09:54:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A perfect ruler would not need to have infinitely many marks or write infinitely many digits to measure a real circle exactly. It could compare lengths directly. For example, take a strip of paper or a flexible tape, wrap it once around the circle, mark where it meets itself, then straighten it. That marked strip is the circumference exactly (idealizing perfect materials). Its length is finite. What you cannot generally do is express that length as a **finite terminating decimal** in units where the diameter is 1. It is \( \pi \) units, and writing its decimal digits never finishes. So there are two separate limitations: - **The length itself:** finite and exact. - **Its decimal numeral in a chosen unit:** endless. A meter stick can have a point exactly \(1/3\) of a meter from one end, even though \(1/3 = 0.333\ldots\) never finishes in decimal. The point and length are complete; decimal notation is not.
