09:55:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see why that feels inseparable: the decimal expansion is how you normally *see* the number written down. But a number and its decimal spelling are not quite the same thing. The spelling is a representation of the number, much like a word is a representation of an object. For instance: \[ 1/3 = 0.3333\ldots \] The decimal expansion is infinite, yet its value is still one-third—not an infinitely large quantity. And: \[ 1 = 0.9999\ldots \] That right-hand expression has endlessly many digits, but it is exactly equal to the finite number 1. So an infinite decimal expansion means the number needs infinitely many decimal places to be written *exactly* in base 10. It does **not** mean the value is infinite in size. All of π’s digits together specify one fixed value between 3 and 4. So you are right that π’s decimal expansion is genuinely infinite. The only correction is that “infinite expansion” and “infinite numerical value” are different properties.
